rs  okay just think about that for a moment  Weald are a red planet and we had  evidence of a river on Mars for the  first time wow talk about fueling your  imagination what is today is not was in  the past  was something completely different and  then when we drilled into the rocks and  we found grey material and realized  there's just red dust everywhere it's  this rust that's all it's iron rust  the whole planets not red that's just  the surface there's something else  hidden underneath all of that and we're  still figuring out what that is I think  I think that is absolutely fascinating  because the challenge is a lot of our  science scientific understanding those  rocks are grey and parts of them turned  red later so what's the story for the  grey rock that's all over the place what  is that that's all we're going to keep  exploring and find out  I helped to put Sam in the operation and  figure out processing of the data so  that we can interpret it one of the big  things that I did was when I first got  to Goddard I was asked to help figure  out how do we calibrate the instrument  once we get the Mars and there's this  little cell on it it's actually no  bigger than the tip of my thumb I like  the size of a thimble and in that we  have a combination of gases and organic  molecules and I had to figure out what  to put in there as a calibrate for all  of the different types of experiments we  intended to do and I had come from  academics I had no idea what I was doing  here I was engineering something that  was critical for the science but I tell  you when the rover launched from Cape  Canaveral and you're looking at this  giant rocket and the very very tippy top  has the rover in it you can barely see  the tippy-top and there's a rover and  they're inside the rover is to say I'm  instrument inside the Sam instrument is  a little I worked on that and it  laughter works and I'll tell you that is  the coolest thing that's a that was a  very special moment and then we used it  once we got the Mars and it was very  important for a lot of the work that we  did the biggest surprise was discovering  that there was evidence of macro  molecules made of carbon these are big  molecules most all natural organic  matter that we find anywhere whether  it's earth biology in meteorites most of  it as macromolecules but we didn't  really expect not everyone expected to  find any evidence of organic matter at  the surface of Mars but the find  evidence of macromolecules gives us a  clue as to how they were preserved and  actually lasted a three and a half  billion  years as they were deposited and all of  the radiation that happened over tens of  millions of years which destroys  organics when we started this mission  most of the people in the science team  did not think we were actually going to  find evidence of organic matter not in  the samples that we were collecting so  to find what we did really makes us all  do a about-turn and changes our  understanding of what's possible  especially when we think about what we  might study in the future if there's  organic molecules preserved at the  surface there most certainly must be  organic molecules preserved deeper down  and they probably preserve the  information that we seek what's it from  is it from a meteorite  it's from geology is it from biology  that's the next big question there's so  much momentum behind this endeavor it's  international there's so many people who  want to see it happen it almost has a  life of its own it's going to happen so  we are working towards NASA is working  towards making that happen there's a lot  of questions that come with that  including science questions and so I'm  I'm very eager to help formulate what  science we will do before we send humans  and once humans are there  the only thing they can be sure of about  the future is it will be absolutely  fantastic  [Music]

